WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.068 --> 00:00:02.350
The happiest place on earth

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:04.720
has been closed for about 10 months,

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.520
and it probably won't be reopening anytime soon.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.180
California isn't allowing theme parks to reopen

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:11.880
until the counties they're located in

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.370
have fewer than one COVID infection

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.970
per a hundred thousand residents for seven consecutive days.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:19.360
The rate here in Orange County

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.927
currently exceeds that by about 90 fold.

00:00:21.927 --> 00:00:23.620
We're gonna be stubborn about it,

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:26.330
California Governor Gavin Newsom said in October.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.610
Newsom's stubbornness is one of a kind.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.770
Every other Disney property in the world has reopened.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:32.670
In California,

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:35.660
some of Disney's retail and dining is allowed to open,

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.170
but the rides and attractions remain closed.

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:41.390
The city of Anaheim meanwhile, looks like a ghost town.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.780
The mouse provides 78,000 jobs

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.030
and is the center of the local economy.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:56.150
Disney world in Orlando opened in July

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:58.380
as Florida's case rates were climbing.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.090
The reopening amounts to a breathtaking effort

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:04.400
by a corporation to prove that it can safely operate

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.170
at a highly dangerous time,

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:07.950
the New York Times observed.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.743
YouTubers created dark parodies of the reopening.

00:01:10.743 --> 00:01:13.070
You don't belong here.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:14.727
But the surge never came.

00:01:14.727 --> 00:01:16.970
This is the tale of two Disneys

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.910
and two radically different approaches

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:22.300
to managing the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.470
You got a lot of people in your profession

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:27.360
who waxed poetically for weeks and weeks

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.957
about how Florida was gonna be just like New York.

00:01:29.957 --> 00:01:32.077
Wait, two weeks, Florida's gonna be next.

00:01:32.077 --> 00:01:33.840
Just like Italy, wait two weeks.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.980
Well, hell we're eight weeks away from that,

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.050
and it hasn't happened.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:38.350
The media savaged

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.030
Florida, governor Ron DeSantis

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.960
when he and a handful of Southern GOP governors

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.613
pushed to reopen most businesses in early summer of 2020.

00:01:46.613 --> 00:01:48.840
DeSantis's strategy from the get go

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:50.960
had been to shield Florida's seniors

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.700
by issuing executive orders

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.390
to temporarily ban nursing home visitations

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.310
and prohibit readmission of COVID positive patients.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:01.500
In contrast to New York governor Andrew Cuomo's order,

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.360
forcing nursing homes to accept COVID patients.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.920
A couple months later with case rates falling,

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:09.487
National Review editor Rich Lowry asks,

00:02:09.487 --> 00:02:12.250
Where does Ron DeSantis go to get his apology?

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.740
Then when cases surged over the summer,

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.430
Los Angeles Times columnist Michael Hiltzik

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.337
delivered on the requested apology.

00:02:19.337 --> 00:02:22.650
Sorry, you're even worse than I imagined, he wrote.

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.250
But by the end of the summer

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.040
cases were falling in Florida

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.030
and rising in areas of the country

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:30.460
that were in full or partial lockdown.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.020
California which had the nation's most stringent policies

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.900
related to COVID-19 began to experience

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.500
a massive second wave in November.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.550
Florida was also seeing a spike in cases and deaths

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.210
though it was less severe.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.940
While the California and Florida

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:48.930
approaches to COVID-19 were vastly different,

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:52.060
their outcomes began to look remarkably similar.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.160
If some in the media had been too quick

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.690
to condemn DeSantis,

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.660
others had also been too quick

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.440
to declare victory for Newsom.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:01.580
In April The Atlantic had extolled

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.660
California's dramatic success

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:06.170
in containing the Corona virus pandemic.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:09.040
What's clear about the impact of Newsom's policies

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:11.280
is that they've taken a devastating toll

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.780
on working-class California residents.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.570
The golden state shed eight percent of its jobs

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.560
compared to five percent in Florida.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.400
It experienced the fifth highest drop in GDP

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.350
among U.S. States and a much higher drop in tax revenue.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:28.640
California now has more total cases per capita

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.780
and is approaching Florida's total deaths per capita,

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.030
despite having a younger population.

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:37.100
California residents have been flouting the lockdowns

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.960
and challenging them in court.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.160
A recall petition for Governor Newsom

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.020
has gathered more than three quarters

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:48.560
of the required 1.5 million signatures to make the ballot.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.265
There are probably 50,000 people

00:03:52.265 --> 00:03:55.490
in a three square mile that are out of work right now,

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.850
due to the restrictions placed by California's governor.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:03:59.700
Here in Anaheim,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.750
Fred Brown has been the general manager

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.310
of Desert Palms Hotel

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:06.830
in the literal shadow of Disneyland for 24 years.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:09.340
So far, he's had to lay off 80 employees

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:10.970
including his own daughter.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.290
If we don't have guests,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.590
I don't know how long some of these hotel owners

00:04:15.590 --> 00:04:18.370
are gonna be able to last without throwing in the towel

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:19.836
and going into bankruptcy.

00:04:19.836 --> 00:04:21.010
I don't think the State of California

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:23.710
has done the best job keeping businesses afloat.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:24.543
Mike Afram

00:04:24.543 --> 00:04:26.610
shut down his shuttle company in March,

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.100
laid off a hundred employees

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:30.530
and has been unable to reopen since.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:31.870
Well, unfortunately I think a lot

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:35.330
of small businesses related to the resort

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.150
or even unrelated to the resort

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:37.983
are not gonna survive,

00:04:37.983 --> 00:04:39.210
or they won't come back.

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:42.150
This is a death knell for tens of thousands of jobs.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:43.800
These are working class jobs

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.510
and hundreds of small businesses in and around the resort.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:49.440
He's usurping precious freedoms away from the private sector

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.180
and increasing our residents and our workers

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.430
reliance on government just to get by.

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:54.410
Trevor O'Neil

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.360
is an Anaheim city council member.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.740
He says tourism accounts

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.670
for almost a hundred million dollars

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.140
of annual tax revenue for Anaheim.

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:03.625
That goes a long way

00:05:03.625 --> 00:05:06.540
to be able to fund our vital city services,

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.070
our public safety, our police and our fire,

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.270
our road repair.

00:05:10.270 --> 00:05:12.390
Everything that a city has to do

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:15.560
and allows us to be able to keep our taxes

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.020
on our residents low.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.550
Disney as a corporation has been very successful

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:21.690
in opening its parks around the world

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:23.200
and including the one in Florida,

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:26.210
and they have proven to know what they're doing

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.870
and know how to do it safely and securely.

00:05:28.870 --> 00:05:30.850
So I don't see why it would be any different

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:33.180
for us here in California or in Anaheim.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.160
Disney is known

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.680
for its expert handling of logistics

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:38.970
and the company drew widespread praise

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.980
when it brought back the NBA playoffs here in Florida,

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.250
by creating a so-called NBA bubble

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.840
that successfully prevented any players or staff

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.530
from COVID infection over a two month span.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.340
California is also home to Universal Studios

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.470
and Six Flags,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.560
in addition to smaller amusement parks

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.180
with no giant corporate backer

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:59.220
to float them through 10 months shut down.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.790
The contrast between California and Florida

00:06:01.790 --> 00:06:03.380
reflects growing evidence

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.570
that lockdowns are not an effective strategy

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:09.770
for managing a viral respiratory epidemic.

00:06:09.770 --> 00:06:10.640
Our estimates show that lockdowns

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.260
have a really dramatic effect in reducing transmission.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:16.130
A June, 2020 paper in Nature,

00:06:16.130 --> 00:06:17.640
claimed that lockdowns would save

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.960
three to four million lives worldwide.

00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:21.830
But it assumed without evidence

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:24.300
that slowing the spread was due to lockdowns

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.280
rather than voluntary behavior,

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.220
and that as lockdowns stretched on

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:29.990
they would remain just as effective

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.110
as in the early days of the pandemic.

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:34.680
After it was published places
with stringent lockdowns,

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.010
including California, The United Kingdom,

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:39.350
and Italy experienced second waves.

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:42.890
A possible theory is that lockdowns become less effective

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.560
over time because the public grows weary

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:48.260
of the social isolation and starts gathering

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:51.010
in private households on a more frequent basis.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.430
According to contact tracing data in New York,

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:57.550
74 percent of cases were contracted inside people's homes.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:06:59.510
You've closed bars, you've closed restaurants

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:01.740
you've closed theaters, you've closed stadiums.

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.010
You've closed mass gatherings.

00:07:04.010 --> 00:07:06.930
Where do people go?

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:09.050
They go home, come to my house.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.020
Big city mayors

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.870
who promoted aggressive lockdowns

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:13.500
are beginning to change tack

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.860
even amidst large caseload's.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:17.470
Chicago's Lori Lightfoot

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.560
and Washington D.C.'s Muriel Bowser

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.600
have pushed for reopening restaurants for indoor dining.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:24.690
And San Francisco and L.A.'s mayors

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:26.820
have allowed outdoor dining to resume.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.870
A January 5th study by a research team at Stanford

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:32.850
compared countries that shut down non-essential businesses

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.930
with ones that took less restrictive public health measures.

00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:37.990
Like only banning large events

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.230
and discouraging international travel.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:41.570
It found no evidence

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:43.410
that the more stringent lockdowns

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:45.020
contributed substantially

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:46.930
to bringing down the case rate.

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.983
Economist Lyman Stone told Reason in May of 2020

00:07:49.983 --> 00:07:52.941
that his study of the lockdowns revealed no correlation

00:07:52.941 --> 00:07:54.660
between the timing of a state

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.800
or nationwide stay at home order

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:58.290
and the spread of the virus.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.260
So we have a couple of different data sources

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:02.500
that basically track your cell phone,

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:03.740
which is a little bit creepy.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.380
And what we can see is that policy choices

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:07.660
like shelter-in-place orders

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:09.230
had almost no impact

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:11.430
on when people began to socially distance.

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:15.190
People were already socially distancing before the lockdown.

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:17.210
Social distancing works.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:18.590
Stone's early findings

00:08:18.590 --> 00:08:20.840
are consistent with the Stanford study,

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:23.170
which found that exposure to information

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:24.850
about the virus and its risks

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:27.810
was a stronger driver of anti-contagion behaviors

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.700
than the specific nature of the government restrictions.

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:33.460
Neither California's statewide restrictions

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.020
nor Florida's more laissez-faire approach

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:39.120
have proven effective at suppressing the virus,

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.960
but various Asian governments and Australia

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:44.880
have for now mostly accomplished that feat,

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.340
and not primarily through lockdowns.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:50.720
For instance, the densely populated city of Hong Kong

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:53.670
has experienced fewer than 200 COVID deaths

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:56.350
out of a population that exceeds 7 million.

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:57.920
But authorities there imposed

00:08:57.920 --> 00:08:59.860
only one short-lived lockdown

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:01.390
in a single neighborhood

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:03.930
experiencing outbreak in mid January.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.960
Stone attributes the overall low case rates

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:09.690
to the city-state's aggressive travel restrictions

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:11.800
and targeted public health measures,

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.370
which were enacted early in the pandemic.

00:09:14.370 --> 00:09:15.720
We should not hesitate

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:18.030
to pull the trigger on closing borders.

00:09:18.030 --> 00:09:20.540
Second, mask early, mask often.

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:21.960
Everyone should wear a mask,

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.440
and they should do so as soon as you know

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:26.530
that you have a threat of a pandemic.

00:09:26.530 --> 00:09:27.960
Information is the most powerful tool

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:29.840
for getting people to understand the risk

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:30.673
and protect themselves.

00:09:30.673 --> 00:09:32.760
But after that, centralized quarantine.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:34.570
Anyone who is a positive test

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:36.950
and any close contact of a positive test,

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:38.960
we need to isolate.

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.880
California governor Gavin Newsom

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.940
lifted his stay at home order on January 25th,

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:47.430
on the grounds ICU's will have the required capacity

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:48.620
in several weeks,

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.910
but the state has withheld some of the data

00:09:50.910 --> 00:09:53.070
they used to make that projection.

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:55.070
Theme parks will remain closed.

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:56.000
In the meantime,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:58.722
Disneyland has opened one of its parking lots

00:09:58.722 --> 00:10:01.900
as Orange County's largest vaccination site.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:03.730
So far, California has had one

00:10:03.730 --> 00:10:07.000
of the slowest vaccine rollouts in the nation.

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:08.710
With the rollout of the vaccine,

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.080
which has not been proven to be the best rollout

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:12.170
compared to other states,

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:14.400
maybe there's a way

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.690
we can responsibly

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.630
sooner open up

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.160
so that we can get back to business

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:23.660
and get the back to surviving.

